codemap 9
tour feature-welcome
kind flow
author ai
group features/gui
note The human, every time they open the window on a repo, often right after an agent session changed the code and the map. Goals: understanding a change you did not write, and code no tour covers being visible. The run before this feature: the window opened on whichever tour sorts first, on this repo the scratch tour !Test/Startup, and nothing on screen said which tours the agent had changed, how much of the code the map covers, how many steps are stale, or that ctrl+p opens the palette. A tabless board from an earlier change showed actions and chords only when no layout was saved, and the layout file is per machine, so it appeared once per machine; it said nothing about the repo and its chords did not line up. The Diff view lists map changes but is a tab you must know to open; the Tours list shows groups but not changes or coverage; Files shows coverage per file, not the whole; the palette needs its chord. Three designs were built and run. A whole-window board on every launch, the saved layout held aside until dismissed: a dismissal every launch, no way back to it, and a mode that keeps a second layout. A Start tab beside Tour: a view kind that is never saved plus a hidden per-panel field for the view it covers, with the first tour still selected behind it. The kept design: the start page is what the Tour view shows when nothing is selected, so it adds no surface and no mode, it follows 'the GUI is one selection', and back or the palette's 'start page' return to it. A launch on this repo shows code-mapper, 168 tours, 51% of symbols covered and 30 stale steps in red, '+ Startup (1 step)' as changed since the parent revision, the top-level groups with their tour counts, the uncovered symbol count, 'Build a tour by hand', and the chords in one aligned column; clicking a change opens that tour, alt+left comes back. On a root with no map it lists entry points, leaving out tests. Building a tour by hand is the guide: a bar that names the next real control and moves on only when that control has been used (the form opens, the tour count grows, a step is added, the step becomes the target, save).

